{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"body":"## Retracted — I filed this against a stale working tree\n\nThis ticket is wrong and should not be worked. The defect it describes is already fixed on `origin/main`.\n\n**The fix that landed:** commit `8c5687af9462dc06ec67b40185bffd2c5989a647` (Gerrit change 2990, \"Reconcile a push-pending marker git disproves\"), carrying trailer `rebar-ticket: 16c9-a42d-fd01-48b9`. On current `origin/main`, `src/rebar/_store/freshness.py` defines `_pending_reconciled(tracker, remote_ref)` at line 129 and calls it at line 223:\n\n```\n222:    elif pending:\n223:        if _pending_reconciled(tracker, remote_ref):\n```\n\nThat is precisely the cross-check this ticket claims is missing.\n\n**How I got it wrong — two compounding errors, both mine:**\n\n1. I read `freshness.py` from the shared main checkout at `/Users/joeoakhart/rebar`, whose HEAD is `e900fa14ad52` — **28 commits behind `origin/main`**. The line I quoted as `freshness.py:155` is genuine, but it is the pre-fix revision.\n2. My corroborating search, `git log --grep='16c9-a42d' --all`, ran **before** `git fetch origin`. The commit was not yet in the local object store, so an empty result looked like confirmation. It was absence of data, not evidence of absence.\n\nThe two errors agreed with each other, which is what made the false conclusion feel solid.\n\n**The symptom I actually experienced was real**, and is now explained without a new defect: every gated write in this session ran the stale build, so it ran the pre-fix code path. rebar told me so directly and I did not act on it — each gate emitted:\n\n```\nWARNING rebar.llm.build_drift: this rebar build (e900fa14a, 2026-09-19, dirty) is\n25 commit(s) BEHIND the ref this gate pinned (6a873c9a9)\n```\n\n**Closing as a duplicate of `16c9-a42d-fd01-48b9`**, linked `duplicates`.\n\n**One genuine finding survives**, recorded here rather than as a new ticket since it is an observation about operating practice, not a code defect: a long-lived agent session working from a checkout that never advances will keep running a build that drifts further from `main` every time it lands a change, and will then read its own stale tree as evidence about current behaviour. The `build_drift` warning already detects this and is labelled advisory. It correctly predicted a wrong conclusion here."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789929317466395001,"uuid":"277b8351-e4bb-4380-8042-e2755721957b"}